WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.404 --> 00:00:01.984
- Hi everyone, Sal Khan here.

00:00:01.984 --> 00:00:04.474
Welcome to the Homeroom live stream.

00:00:04.474 --> 00:00:06.084
We have a very exciting guest today.

00:00:06.084 --> 00:00:07.654
We're gonna have John Dickerson,

00:00:07.654 --> 00:00:11.504
who is, works for 60 Minutes, CBS

00:00:11.504 --> 00:00:15.615
contributing analyst,
contributes to the Atlantic

00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:19.214
and also has written, "The
Hardest Job In The World."

00:00:19.214 --> 00:00:20.504
So a lot to talk about, especially

00:00:20.504 --> 00:00:23.283
with the election coming up
and kind of our government

00:00:23.283 --> 00:00:24.664
and the history of United States.

00:00:24.664 --> 00:00:25.954
If you have any questions about that,

00:00:25.954 --> 00:00:28.524
feel free to start putting
them in the message boards,

00:00:28.524 --> 00:00:29.594
wherever you are watching this.

00:00:29.594 --> 00:00:31.444
Then our team members
are going to surface it

00:00:31.444 --> 00:00:33.024
to myself and John.

00:00:33.024 --> 00:00:35.634
But before we jump into that
very exciting conversation,

00:00:35.634 --> 00:00:37.104
I'll give my standard reminders.

00:00:37.104 --> 00:00:38.954
First of all, reminder that Khan Academy

00:00:38.954 --> 00:00:40.675
is a not-for-profit organization

00:00:40.675 --> 00:00:44.554
and we can only do what we
do with philanthropic support

00:00:44.554 --> 00:00:45.764
from folks like yourself.

00:00:45.764 --> 00:00:47.744
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:47.744 --> 00:00:51.114
please think about going
to Khanacademy.org/donate

00:00:51.114 --> 00:00:52.924
and making a donation.

00:00:52.924 --> 00:00:54.604
Also want to give a special shout out

00:00:54.604 --> 00:00:58.684
to several organizations that
have stepped up during COVID.

00:00:58.684 --> 00:01:00.344
When they realized that
we were already running

00:01:00.344 --> 00:01:03.337
a deficit before COVID
and that deficit only grew

00:01:03.337 --> 00:01:06.214
as we had to have higher server costs

00:01:06.214 --> 00:01:07.884
and accelerate a whole bunch of programs.

00:01:07.884 --> 00:01:09.834
So special thanks to Bank Of America,

00:01:09.834 --> 00:01:12.157
Google.org, AT&amp;T, Fastly, Novartis

00:01:12.157 --> 00:01:14.044
and the many, many other supporters

00:01:14.044 --> 00:01:15.994
that have supported Khan
Academy through the years.

00:01:15.994 --> 00:01:17.904
And especially all of
y'all who've continued

00:01:17.904 --> 00:01:19.184
to support us through COVID.

00:01:19.184 --> 00:01:21.624
It's the only way we can do what we do.

00:01:21.624 --> 00:01:23.584
And the last announcement,

00:01:23.584 --> 00:01:25.844
just reminding everyone
that you can get a version

00:01:25.844 --> 00:01:28.169
of this live stream in podcast format

00:01:28.169 --> 00:01:30.304
or wherever you get your podcasts,

00:01:30.304 --> 00:01:32.217
Homeroom with Sal, the podcast.

00:01:32.217 --> 00:01:37.217
So with that I'm excited to
introduce John Dickerson,

00:01:37.574 --> 00:01:41.364
60 Minutes correspondent,
CBS contributing analysts.

00:01:41.364 --> 00:01:42.614
You contribute to the Atlantic.

00:01:42.614 --> 00:01:43.464
You do a lot of things.

00:01:43.464 --> 00:01:45.471
I mean, any one of the things
I just mentioned would be

00:01:45.471 --> 00:01:49.764
more than a full-time job,
but good to see you John.

00:01:49.764 --> 00:01:51.664
- It's great to be with you again Sal.

00:01:52.984 --> 00:01:55.754
- So maybe a good place to
start, I have a ton of questions,

00:01:55.754 --> 00:01:59.065
but obviously we're days
away from an election.

00:01:59.065 --> 00:02:02.174
You know every election I
think always feels special

00:02:02.174 --> 00:02:03.814
and different, but this one feels

00:02:03.814 --> 00:02:05.554
more different than normal.

00:02:05.554 --> 00:02:08.624
Obviously it's happening in
very unusual circumstances.

00:02:08.624 --> 00:02:10.787
How are you thinking about it?

00:02:11.745 --> 00:02:13.364
- Well, I think you're right.

00:02:13.364 --> 00:02:16.564
There's, so the most the basic
reason it feels different

00:02:16.564 --> 00:02:17.854
is that we are holding

00:02:17.854 --> 00:02:20.764
or trying to hold an election
in the time of a pandemic.

00:02:20.764 --> 00:02:23.324
So that every vote tasked and counted

00:02:23.324 --> 00:02:26.054
is under different scrutiny state.

00:02:26.054 --> 00:02:30.244
Some States have had to create
entirely new ways of voting.

00:02:30.244 --> 00:02:33.324
So that would be, just be
the basic big challenge

00:02:33.324 --> 00:02:35.074
of the moment where in.

00:02:35.074 --> 00:02:37.404
We are also in an highly
contentious moment

00:02:37.404 --> 00:02:39.494
in which the president who
is usually the guardian

00:02:39.494 --> 00:02:41.564
and steward of that system,

00:02:41.564 --> 00:02:43.604
which is the bedrock
of the American system,

00:02:43.604 --> 00:02:47.854
which is a form for voting that
allows the peaceful transfer

00:02:47.854 --> 00:02:49.994
of power or the peaceful
maintenance of power.

00:02:49.994 --> 00:02:52.114
It's basically the first block

00:02:52.114 --> 00:02:54.214
in the building block
of American government.

00:02:54.214 --> 00:02:57.084
The president has been
questioning, challenging,

00:02:57.084 --> 00:02:59.944
poking, prodding at that basic brick

00:02:59.944 --> 00:03:02.344
in the structure of America,

00:03:02.344 --> 00:03:06.174
by questioning whether
the votes will be honest,

00:03:06.174 --> 00:03:09.244
whether the vote counting
and the way it's being done

00:03:09.244 --> 00:03:12.052
is somehow gonna throw
the result into question.

00:03:12.052 --> 00:03:15.464
So you have the voting in a pandemic,

00:03:15.464 --> 00:03:18.654
and then you have the, basically
the most popular well-known

00:03:18.654 --> 00:03:22.254
figure in American politics
calling that into question.

00:03:22.254 --> 00:03:23.964
So those are just two things.

00:03:23.964 --> 00:03:25.884
And then of course you
have a series of issues

00:03:25.884 --> 00:03:27.624
that are at the center of the election,

00:03:27.624 --> 00:03:31.114
which are major and big
and need to be addressed.

00:03:31.114 --> 00:03:34.344
Pandemic is one, the economic
situation in America is other,

00:03:34.344 --> 00:03:35.789
is the other.

00:03:35.789 --> 00:03:39.614
And then the ongoing
racial debate questions,

00:03:39.614 --> 00:03:42.274
protests about equality in America

00:03:42.274 --> 00:03:46.214
and whether that is broadly
shared or broadly available to,

00:03:46.214 --> 00:03:48.137
and particularly to Black Americans.

00:03:49.394 --> 00:03:50.227
- And how do you think,

00:03:50.227 --> 00:03:52.884
I know predicting the future
is always a hard thing to do,

00:03:52.884 --> 00:03:54.584
but let's try a little bit.

00:03:54.584 --> 00:03:55.814
How do you think it's going to play out?

00:03:55.814 --> 00:03:58.103
Given everything you just talked about,

00:03:58.103 --> 00:04:01.044
on election day, this isn't
gonna be a typical election day,

00:04:01.044 --> 00:04:03.141
whereby midnight or 1:00
AM people are gonna say,

00:04:03.141 --> 00:04:06.101
"Oh it looks like, so-and-so
is the next president."

00:04:07.271 --> 00:04:09.134
- Here's what I'm looking for in terms

00:04:09.134 --> 00:04:10.124
of the way it plays out.

00:04:10.124 --> 00:04:11.754
The one thing to watch is right now,

00:04:11.754 --> 00:04:13.394
the early vote that's taking place

00:04:13.394 --> 00:04:15.941
both in person and by mail.

00:04:15.941 --> 00:04:19.474
There were some States
like Florida and Arizona

00:04:19.474 --> 00:04:21.784
that start the counting early.

00:04:21.784 --> 00:04:24.484
And those are States that we
should all kind of look to

00:04:24.484 --> 00:04:26.224
on election night.

00:04:26.224 --> 00:04:28.504
I become particularly
interested in Arizona.

00:04:28.504 --> 00:04:31.034
They start counting two weeks before,

00:04:31.034 --> 00:04:33.594
they've been voting early since 1996.

00:04:33.594 --> 00:04:35.144
So they're pretty good at doing it.

00:04:35.144 --> 00:04:37.054
About 90% of the vote will be counted

00:04:37.054 --> 00:04:39.884
before election day in Arizona,

00:04:39.884 --> 00:04:42.223
which means you're gonna have durable,

00:04:42.223 --> 00:04:45.323
a durable counting process there.

00:04:45.323 --> 00:04:49.044
And so we're gonna, so we
have the early vote going on,

00:04:49.044 --> 00:04:51.304
then we look at the States
that are able to process those

00:04:51.304 --> 00:04:54.044
votes so that we'll get an
answer on election night.

00:04:54.044 --> 00:04:55.334
And then we've gotta look at the votes

00:04:55.334 --> 00:04:57.134
that are being cast on election day.

00:04:57.134 --> 00:04:58.894
And one of the splits
people should look for

00:04:58.894 --> 00:05:01.294
is that a lot of Democrats
are voting early.

00:05:01.294 --> 00:05:03.644
It's likely that the bulk
of the Republican vote

00:05:03.644 --> 00:05:05.624
will be on election day.

00:05:05.624 --> 00:05:08.004
So we should all be
prepared for some numbers

00:05:08.004 --> 00:05:09.656
that come in that show,

00:05:09.656 --> 00:05:12.824
perhaps Joe Biden winning
because of the early vote,

00:05:12.824 --> 00:05:15.364
but then maybe change once some States

00:05:15.364 --> 00:05:18.614
start to count their
in-person vote during the day.

00:05:18.614 --> 00:05:20.350
And we should all be ready and prepared

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:22.234
if it's a close election,

00:05:22.234 --> 00:05:24.879
for the election to be determined maybe

00:05:24.879 --> 00:05:27.264
into the next day after election day,

00:05:27.264 --> 00:05:30.024
or maybe even some days down beyond that.

00:05:30.024 --> 00:05:32.114
On the other hand, early in the evening,

00:05:32.114 --> 00:05:35.134
we'll get from some East
coast States, Florida,

00:05:35.134 --> 00:05:39.124
North Carolina in particular,
Georgia that will tell us

00:05:39.124 --> 00:05:40.924
something about the way
the night is trending.

00:05:40.924 --> 00:05:43.284
And it could be, even
though everybody is prepared

00:05:43.284 --> 00:05:45.414
for a long election week,

00:05:45.414 --> 00:05:47.784
it could be that we get a
signal early in the night

00:05:47.784 --> 00:05:50.891
from some of those States
that it's gonna go one way,

00:05:50.891 --> 00:05:53.070
because the turnout is just,

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.994
being for one candidate or another,

00:05:55.994 --> 00:05:57.544
and we could get an early result.

00:05:57.544 --> 00:06:00.552
So everybody's preparing
for a long counting process,

00:06:00.552 --> 00:06:03.214
but that doesn't mean that's
the only kind of answer

00:06:03.214 --> 00:06:05.757
we might get once the votes
started being counted.

00:06:06.954 --> 00:06:08.594
- And I just wanna make sure
I understand the logistics

00:06:08.594 --> 00:06:11.424
or how the information gets out
as we approach election day.

00:06:11.424 --> 00:06:12.494
So even in a place like Arizona,

00:06:12.494 --> 00:06:14.728
where they start the
counting ahead of time,

00:06:14.728 --> 00:06:17.414
do they release that
information as they have it?

00:06:17.414 --> 00:06:18.784
Is that where we see like,

00:06:18.784 --> 00:06:20.204
the precincts reporting and all that.

00:06:20.204 --> 00:06:22.254
How does it get out there?

00:06:22.254 --> 00:06:25.374
- This is my favorite fact
about Arizona at the moment,

00:06:25.374 --> 00:06:28.874
the counting is going on and
it is in a computer somewhere.

00:06:28.874 --> 00:06:30.694
The tabulation, so what they do is that

00:06:30.694 --> 00:06:33.854
they open the ballot,
they check the information

00:06:33.854 --> 00:06:35.344
on the ballot against the voter file.

00:06:35.344 --> 00:06:37.034
They check the signatures,
they make sure it's

00:06:37.034 --> 00:06:41.584
an actual valid ballot
and then they tabulate it.

00:06:41.584 --> 00:06:44.074
Those tabulations are
sitting in a computer

00:06:44.074 --> 00:06:47.634
and we'll sit in a computer
until election day.

00:06:47.634 --> 00:06:49.311
It's not hooked up to the internet.

00:06:49.311 --> 00:06:52.182
It is a number inside the
computer that nobody knows.

00:06:52.182 --> 00:06:55.434
Then once the polls close
in Arizona on election day,

00:06:55.434 --> 00:06:57.164
they will release that tabulation.

00:06:57.164 --> 00:06:59.824
So that's the tabulation of the early mail

00:06:59.824 --> 00:07:01.494
and in-person vote.

00:07:01.494 --> 00:07:04.758
And so you'll get a big
kind of gurgle of results.

00:07:04.758 --> 00:07:07.414
And then slowly over time,

00:07:07.414 --> 00:07:09.994
they will add in the
votes on election day.

00:07:09.994 --> 00:07:13.044
And so those votes by
machine on election day,

00:07:13.044 --> 00:07:14.854
but then also some people will drop off

00:07:14.854 --> 00:07:16.074
their ballots on election day,

00:07:16.074 --> 00:07:18.584
which means that they've
gotten an early valid,

00:07:18.584 --> 00:07:20.574
and then it will have to be assessed.

00:07:20.574 --> 00:07:21.864
They'll compare the signatures,

00:07:21.864 --> 00:07:23.524
they'll compare it to the voter roll.

00:07:23.524 --> 00:07:25.244
And so that'll take a little bit of time,

00:07:25.244 --> 00:07:27.644
Arizona even though
they have a time tested,

00:07:27.644 --> 00:07:31.684
well stressed vote by mail process.

00:07:31.684 --> 00:07:34.974
They've had some elections
recently where the result on

00:07:34.974 --> 00:07:37.174
election night has been different
than the results several

00:07:37.174 --> 00:07:40.934
days later because of
that counting process.

00:07:40.934 --> 00:07:42.354
Now that's just in Arizona.

00:07:42.354 --> 00:07:44.774
Every state has a different process.

00:07:44.774 --> 00:07:47.751
It's why some of the States
people are most fascinated by

00:07:47.751 --> 00:07:50.644
Pennsylvania, Michigan, and Wisconsin.

00:07:50.644 --> 00:07:53.574
Those are three States that
Donald Trump won in 2016,

00:07:53.574 --> 00:07:56.004
which was a part of his
surprise, upset victory.

00:07:56.004 --> 00:07:58.224
Those are, I call them the headache States

00:07:58.224 --> 00:08:01.414
because Pennsylvania doesn't
start processing ballots

00:08:01.414 --> 00:08:02.874
until election day.

00:08:02.874 --> 00:08:05.020
So their process is kind of slow

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:07.414
and it may mean that
we don't get a result.

00:08:07.414 --> 00:08:08.914
If those States are close,

00:08:08.914 --> 00:08:10.954
you may not get a result for a few days.

00:08:10.954 --> 00:08:13.434
And there will be lots
of things to bicker over

00:08:13.434 --> 00:08:15.824
in the way that they count their ballots.

00:08:15.824 --> 00:08:19.386
So every state does it differently,
some better than others.

00:08:19.386 --> 00:08:22.264
- And a lot of questions
are coming in related

00:08:22.264 --> 00:08:23.374
to what you just talked about

00:08:23.374 --> 00:08:24.921
from YouTube Mark R. Thompson says,

00:08:24.921 --> 00:08:28.256
"why can't all States do
what Arizona is doing?"

00:08:28.256 --> 00:08:33.224
- (laughs) Well we may that
they may all start to try

00:08:33.224 --> 00:08:34.454
and do that.

00:08:34.454 --> 00:08:38.274
The principle has been that local control,

00:08:38.274 --> 00:08:42.274
in the United States where
this is best handled by people

00:08:42.274 --> 00:08:44.374
closest to the ones
doing the actual voting.

00:08:44.374 --> 00:08:45.914
And the upside also of it is

00:08:45.914 --> 00:08:48.334
when we think about hacking and disruption

00:08:48.334 --> 00:08:52.243
from foreign adversaries
or anybody who would try

00:08:52.243 --> 00:08:56.384
to seize power in America
from a local perspective,

00:08:56.384 --> 00:08:59.104
very hard to do when you have many,

00:08:59.104 --> 00:09:01.577
many different kinds of voting systems.

00:09:01.577 --> 00:09:05.044
And so in a way, the
inefficiency of having

00:09:05.044 --> 00:09:07.844
so many different systems
has a slight benefit in that

00:09:07.844 --> 00:09:09.414
it's harder to disrupt.

00:09:09.414 --> 00:09:12.114
Because you'd have to learn how
to disrupt each little play,

00:09:12.114 --> 00:09:13.845
each little County sometimes.

00:09:13.845 --> 00:09:17.109
And in some States, the rules
are different by County.

00:09:17.109 --> 00:09:20.189
So there's a way in which the inefficiency

00:09:20.189 --> 00:09:22.038
actually protects us a little bit

00:09:22.038 --> 00:09:27.038
but and I should just add,
we have tried as a country

00:09:27.104 --> 00:09:31.104
to reform our voting process
during the Ford administration,

00:09:31.104 --> 00:09:33.564
Carter administration,
Obama administration,

00:09:33.564 --> 00:09:36.211
and they are big binders full of analysis

00:09:36.211 --> 00:09:38.754
on how to make voting easier and better

00:09:38.754 --> 00:09:42.362
and it never quite happens
for variety of reasons in part

00:09:42.362 --> 00:09:44.704
because the federal government
can't tell the States

00:09:44.704 --> 00:09:45.904
what to do.

00:09:45.904 --> 00:09:50.904
But maybe after the embarrassing
delays and confusion here,

00:09:51.124 --> 00:09:53.383
there might be, maybe
there'll be a new push

00:09:53.383 --> 00:09:55.817
to improve things.

00:09:57.404 --> 00:09:58.237
- I like that point.

00:09:58.237 --> 00:10:00.065
That was the first time I
heard kind of our fragmentation

00:10:00.065 --> 00:10:03.752
of our voting system as
a feature and not a bug.

00:10:03.752 --> 00:10:05.041
But it doesn't make sense.

00:10:05.041 --> 00:10:06.904
It does make it harder to
game because you have to

00:10:06.904 --> 00:10:08.804
game it at at least at a state level,

00:10:08.804 --> 00:10:10.274
probably at a local level.

00:10:10.274 --> 00:10:12.004
And that goes into another question,

00:10:12.004 --> 00:10:16.420
you alluded to the fact that
Donald Trump has said a few

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:20.094
times that he's skeptical
of some of the mail-in early

00:10:20.094 --> 00:10:22.150
voting that may be the election

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:24.054
might be called into question

00:10:24.054 --> 00:10:25.471
from YouTube, Mr. Spector's asking,

00:10:25.471 --> 00:10:26.934
"will it be a fair election?

00:10:26.934 --> 00:10:29.474
How will they make sure the votes are fair

00:10:29.474 --> 00:10:30.721
and nothing is rigged?"

00:10:31.994 --> 00:10:33.381
- Well each of the individual States

00:10:33.381 --> 00:10:35.886
and the secretaries of
States in the States will,

00:10:35.886 --> 00:10:38.340
has a process for doing that.

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:41.874
Part one thing people should
really keep in mind is that,

00:10:41.874 --> 00:10:43.664
a lot of these States
and particularly ones

00:10:43.664 --> 00:10:45.644
that have close elections,

00:10:45.644 --> 00:10:49.764
have a time tested an honored
way of figuring out whether

00:10:49.764 --> 00:10:51.074
there's any fraud.

00:10:51.074 --> 00:10:53.404
The reason there is so little fraud

00:10:53.404 --> 00:10:58.084
is it's really hard to do and
when they have found fraud

00:10:58.084 --> 00:11:01.614
on election day or through
voting, it's been found out.

00:11:01.614 --> 00:11:06.614
So it might take a little
while in some States

00:11:06.694 --> 00:11:09.274
because they have a kind of
multi-level process to make sure

00:11:09.274 --> 00:11:13.094
that the voting that's taking
place is honorable and true.

00:11:13.094 --> 00:11:15.944
But the length of time
that it's taking means

00:11:15.944 --> 00:11:19.874
that they are doing their work.

00:11:19.874 --> 00:11:21.821
They are checking their,

00:11:21.821 --> 00:11:23.694
they're doing their math again,

00:11:23.694 --> 00:11:25.704
they're not rushing through the process.

00:11:25.704 --> 00:11:29.498
And so delay and it might
be a little irritating

00:11:29.498 --> 00:11:32.559
in this world where we
would all like everything

00:11:32.559 --> 00:11:36.804
instantaneously, but it is
the sign of diligent work

00:11:36.804 --> 00:11:39.304
going forward that is
actually gonna protect

00:11:39.304 --> 00:11:41.204
the durability of the vote.

00:11:41.204 --> 00:11:44.285
So what we can rely on is
the systems within the States

00:11:44.285 --> 00:11:47.844
and the fact that everybody's
been worried about

00:11:47.844 --> 00:11:50.064
what voting in a pandemic
is gonna be like.

00:11:50.064 --> 00:11:52.328
And so there are a lot of
people who are watching

00:11:52.328 --> 00:11:57.244
and while some States have
had rigorous debates over

00:11:57.244 --> 00:11:58.794
the number of polling places

00:11:58.794 --> 00:12:02.104
and the number of drop-offs
spots for early ballots,

00:12:02.104 --> 00:12:04.224
there are a lot of
people paying attention.

00:12:04.224 --> 00:12:06.904
And so it's a little bit
harder to do a slight of hand

00:12:06.904 --> 00:12:08.254
or some kind of fraud

00:12:08.254 --> 00:12:11.217
with so many eyes on what's taking place.

00:12:12.754 --> 00:12:14.534
- And, one thing that you brought up

00:12:14.534 --> 00:12:17.720
and that you talking about
that gives me confidence

00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:19.474
in what is happening.

00:12:19.474 --> 00:12:20.974
But even in your kind of,

00:12:20.974 --> 00:12:22.454
it sounds like almost not worst case,

00:12:22.454 --> 00:12:24.014
but not great scenarios.

00:12:24.014 --> 00:12:26.734
You have it maybe a few
days past election day.

00:12:26.734 --> 00:12:28.254
I was hearing these scenarios,

00:12:28.254 --> 00:12:29.861
I haven't heard them as much
recently where people think

00:12:29.861 --> 00:12:31.944
"it might take weeks
and it might be disputed

00:12:31.944 --> 00:12:35.604
and it might go, depending
on how it's disputed,

00:12:35.604 --> 00:12:37.089
if some States don't wanna say

00:12:37.089 --> 00:12:39.404
that they've have a final tally,

00:12:39.404 --> 00:12:40.724
that it could go to
the state legislatures.

00:12:40.724 --> 00:12:42.834
I've been hearing all of
these other kinds of Byzantine

00:12:42.834 --> 00:12:43.984
worst-case scenarios.

00:12:43.984 --> 00:12:45.824
Do you think any of those are
happening or is that just kind

00:12:45.824 --> 00:12:49.057
of scaremongering of like an
absolute disaster scenario?

00:12:50.274 --> 00:12:51.964
- Well it's a good question.

00:12:51.964 --> 00:12:55.744
I could cook up some
truly awful scenarios.

00:12:55.744 --> 00:13:00.214
I mean basically the
closer it is, the more

00:13:02.033 --> 00:13:03.954
Turkey it might get.

00:13:03.954 --> 00:13:07.091
Because a, if it's closed, it means that

00:13:07.091 --> 00:13:10.884
every tiny little vote
really could matter.

00:13:10.884 --> 00:13:13.364
Remember what happened in 2000 in Florida,

00:13:13.364 --> 00:13:16.794
you had a committee of
people obsessing about

00:13:16.794 --> 00:13:19.919
the tiny pieces of paper that
hung from the punch ballots,

00:13:19.919 --> 00:13:22.226
the chads as you'll remember.

00:13:22.226 --> 00:13:26.344
Which really showed how you
can funnel the entire intensity

00:13:26.344 --> 00:13:27.924
of the American political system

00:13:27.924 --> 00:13:30.194
and the power that is
exchanged in elections

00:13:30.194 --> 00:13:32.501
down to a tiny piece of paper that is

00:13:32.501 --> 00:13:35.924
basically barely bigger
than the head of a pencil.

00:13:35.924 --> 00:13:38.521
So it can get really weird, really fast,

00:13:38.521 --> 00:13:41.394
and if it's not close however,

00:13:41.394 --> 00:13:43.944
then there might be some sloppiness.

00:13:43.944 --> 00:13:45.314
There always is in an election.

00:13:45.314 --> 00:13:46.754
It's a massive undertaking.

00:13:46.754 --> 00:13:49.081
We might have, you know, between 150,

00:13:49.081 --> 00:13:51.374
160 million people in America.

00:13:51.374 --> 00:13:53.454
It is not something that ever goes off

00:13:53.454 --> 00:13:55.398
with a hundred percent purity

00:13:55.398 --> 00:13:58.814
just because of human
activity at that scale

00:13:58.814 --> 00:14:01.354
has bumps and messiness

00:14:01.354 --> 00:14:04.954
and an occasional untucked shirt tail.

00:14:04.954 --> 00:14:06.755
But if it's not close,

00:14:06.755 --> 00:14:10.354
a little messiness can be
accommodated by the overwhelming

00:14:10.354 --> 00:14:12.721
result for one of the two candidates.

00:14:12.721 --> 00:14:14.275
And I think that it,

00:14:14.275 --> 00:14:18.534
so basically I've tried not
to sketch the total disaster

00:14:18.534 --> 00:14:20.964
scenarios because while
people are prepared for them

00:14:20.964 --> 00:14:25.543
and everybody should be emotionally
prepared for complexity.

00:14:25.543 --> 00:14:30.044
I also think if we get to up on tiptoe

00:14:30.044 --> 00:14:34.411
and get to talk too much about
how awful it all could be,

00:14:34.411 --> 00:14:37.944
it sets the stage for mischief makers

00:14:37.944 --> 00:14:40.094
who some of whom are domestic,

00:14:40.094 --> 00:14:42.192
some of whom are from our adversaries.

00:14:42.192 --> 00:14:47.134
Who in a highly heightened and
atmosphere can throw in just

00:14:47.134 --> 00:14:49.874
a little this information
and if everybody's already

00:14:49.874 --> 00:14:52.724
so keyed up that little bit of information

00:14:52.724 --> 00:14:55.454
will cause people to go
pinballing all over the place.

00:14:55.454 --> 00:14:59.084
And so one of our jobs in the press

00:14:59.084 --> 00:15:01.604
is to try to keep the eye on the ball,

00:15:01.604 --> 00:15:03.538
what's the most important thing

00:15:03.538 --> 00:15:06.403
and not go through a
kind of doom scrolling

00:15:06.403 --> 00:15:09.554
of all the possible, awful
things that might happen.

00:15:09.554 --> 00:15:14.554
And in fact, teach people
or remind people that,

00:15:15.371 --> 00:15:18.314
this is going to be a developing
story on election night

00:15:18.314 --> 00:15:23.314
and to ignore rumors and
to practice some restraint

00:15:23.624 --> 00:15:25.758
in handling the incoming information.

00:15:25.758 --> 00:15:29.155
Because it might take
a slightly winding path

00:15:29.155 --> 00:15:32.319
and don't mistake the natural winding

00:15:32.319 --> 00:15:34.254
for the electro counting process

00:15:34.254 --> 00:15:36.354
for anything having gone wrong.

00:15:36.354 --> 00:15:39.567
If they do that, there will
be less chaos on election day.

00:15:41.154 --> 00:15:43.234
- You're touching on
actually the next question

00:15:43.234 --> 00:15:44.644
that has come through on this notion

00:15:44.644 --> 00:15:47.534
of foreign influence mischief maker,

00:15:47.534 --> 00:15:48.864
polarization from Facebook.

00:15:48.864 --> 00:15:50.171
John Lavelle asks,

00:15:50.171 --> 00:15:51.724
"the foreign influence
that has taken place

00:15:51.724 --> 00:15:53.454
in our country has fueled
the division that threatens

00:15:53.454 --> 00:15:55.454
the union and has been very successful.

00:15:55.454 --> 00:15:57.604
The two tribes are at each other's throat.

00:15:57.604 --> 00:15:59.804
The question is who has
helped widen the gap?"

00:15:59.804 --> 00:16:01.174
And I'll expand that a little bit.

00:16:01.174 --> 00:16:03.804
I do sometimes wonder how successful have

00:16:03.804 --> 00:16:06.204
the foreign influence actually been?

00:16:06.204 --> 00:16:07.944
It seems like there's evidence
that it's been going on,

00:16:07.944 --> 00:16:09.424
but how successful has it been?

00:16:09.424 --> 00:16:10.594
Or has it been more of our

00:16:10.594 --> 00:16:14.049
own making through
polarizations or social media,

00:16:14.049 --> 00:16:18.965
or even the media itself
changing and kind of polarizing?

00:16:18.965 --> 00:16:21.041
- Well the answer to that question is yes,

00:16:21.041 --> 00:16:24.784
which is to say that certainly
we had foreign adversaries

00:16:24.784 --> 00:16:28.204
who were trying through a
variety of different methods,

00:16:28.204 --> 00:16:33.204
Facebook groups, phony ads,
attempts at voter suppression

00:16:33.344 --> 00:16:35.984
to tinker with American politics.

00:16:35.984 --> 00:16:39.274
But I think one of the
most potentially powerful

00:16:39.274 --> 00:16:41.334
things they did was

00:16:42.674 --> 00:16:46.684
in a nation where political
combat is so corrosive

00:16:46.684 --> 00:16:51.254
among the highly online
and the highly engaged,

00:16:51.254 --> 00:16:53.054
which is not the majority of our country

00:16:53.054 --> 00:16:55.233
and it's not even the majority
of voters, but nevertheless

00:16:55.233 --> 00:16:57.830
it does represent a lot
of people on social media

00:16:57.830 --> 00:17:00.982
who can affect news
coverage, who can affect

00:17:00.982 --> 00:17:04.369
the culture in which voting takes place.

00:17:04.369 --> 00:17:06.914
That's where when you throw disinformation

00:17:06.914 --> 00:17:09.503
into the middle of that kind of swarm,

00:17:09.503 --> 00:17:13.224
where we're already
bickering and short-tempered

00:17:13.224 --> 00:17:16.962
and questioning motives
in our political debate,

00:17:16.962 --> 00:17:21.014
it has an explosive effect.

00:17:21.014 --> 00:17:25.694
So it's not, in some cases
it's not even misinformation.

00:17:25.694 --> 00:17:30.084
It's just the coarsest
possible information,

00:17:30.084 --> 00:17:32.774
which then causes a reaction
and a counter reaction

00:17:32.774 --> 00:17:34.454
from each side.

00:17:34.454 --> 00:17:39.454
So the partisanship in America
among political obsessives

00:17:40.587 --> 00:17:42.914
has gotten much, much worse,

00:17:42.914 --> 00:17:44.804
both in the way they
think about the parties

00:17:44.804 --> 00:17:46.004
and the way in which they think about

00:17:46.004 --> 00:17:48.141
the opposite members of the party.

00:17:48.141 --> 00:17:50.684
As you know about the
polling that long ago people

00:17:50.684 --> 00:17:53.774
who were political
obsessives would be okay

00:17:53.774 --> 00:17:56.634
with one of their
offspring marrying somebody

00:17:56.634 --> 00:17:58.334
of the other party.

00:17:58.334 --> 00:18:00.414
Now that number is down.

00:18:00.414 --> 00:18:03.774
It might be in the teens
of political obsessives,

00:18:03.774 --> 00:18:05.904
who would be all right with
one of their offspring,

00:18:05.904 --> 00:18:07.974
marrying a member of the other party.

00:18:07.974 --> 00:18:09.574
Those are the political obsessives.

00:18:09.574 --> 00:18:12.229
There's also a lot of
work that's done on voters

00:18:12.229 --> 00:18:15.545
that finds that basically
about 80% of the voters

00:18:15.545 --> 00:18:19.274
are not obsessed and
thinking the absolute worst

00:18:19.274 --> 00:18:20.734
of the other party.

00:18:20.734 --> 00:18:23.034
Now they can get there pretty fast.

00:18:23.034 --> 00:18:26.477
If you get into a conversation
with them and you misbehave

00:18:26.477 --> 00:18:30.217
and you treat them as,

00:18:30.217 --> 00:18:33.054
subhuman because of the
party they belong to.

00:18:33.054 --> 00:18:35.584
But mostly the people
who spend a lot of time

00:18:35.584 --> 00:18:38.134
on social media and the people who are,

00:18:38.134 --> 00:18:40.494
who represent the worst
in our political dialogue

00:18:40.494 --> 00:18:42.442
are actually a relatively small percentage

00:18:42.442 --> 00:18:46.144
of the electorate, and an
even smaller percentage

00:18:46.144 --> 00:18:47.294
of the American people.

00:18:48.564 --> 00:18:52.064
- And, we this polarization,

00:18:52.064 --> 00:18:53.184
where you think it's gonna lead to?

00:18:53.184 --> 00:18:55.184
Do you think it depends on
the outcome of the election?

00:18:55.184 --> 00:18:56.827
Do you think we have some just,

00:18:56.827 --> 00:18:59.424
structural trends because of social media

00:18:59.424 --> 00:19:01.784
and it's not like the
Russians and the Iranians

00:19:01.784 --> 00:19:04.404
and the Chinese are gonna
disappear overnight?

00:19:04.404 --> 00:19:07.010
I mean, is there some way
that we converge back to,

00:19:07.010 --> 00:19:11.031
and is there any lessons from
history that tell us that

00:19:11.031 --> 00:19:12.364
"okay we can clearly,

00:19:12.364 --> 00:19:13.934
I mean obviously we've had a civil war,

00:19:13.934 --> 00:19:16.124
so things have gotten bad in the past."

00:19:16.124 --> 00:19:18.424
How do we heal?

00:19:18.424 --> 00:19:20.654
What's it gonna look like in 20 years?

00:19:20.654 --> 00:19:23.307
- Yeah well the civil war example

00:19:23.307 --> 00:19:25.694
is the most dangerous one of course,

00:19:25.694 --> 00:19:28.988
which is that we only heal
after we tear ourselves apart.

00:19:28.988 --> 00:19:33.988
And so you'd hate to have
to see reform come out

00:19:35.214 --> 00:19:36.884
of that kind of hot stove moment

00:19:37.724 --> 00:19:39.754
because of the carnage that would be

00:19:39.754 --> 00:19:42.324
required to create that
kind of wake up call

00:19:42.324 --> 00:19:44.064
for the whole country.

00:19:44.064 --> 00:19:47.994
There are, I mean I
think the best case for

00:19:49.564 --> 00:19:52.102
getting out of our current fix

00:19:52.102 --> 00:19:54.814
is probably a combination of two things.

00:19:54.814 --> 00:19:58.695
One would be a considerable
political defeat

00:19:58.695 --> 00:20:01.594
of one of the two parties,

00:20:01.594 --> 00:20:04.274
which would change the
political incentives

00:20:04.274 --> 00:20:06.824
for the individual members in the party

00:20:06.824 --> 00:20:09.594
to not listen to the base of their party

00:20:09.594 --> 00:20:11.494
but listen to the larger country.

00:20:11.494 --> 00:20:14.844
The problem is and I'm
gonna be general here,

00:20:14.844 --> 00:20:17.364
although, at the moment,

00:20:17.364 --> 00:20:18.814
because Donald Trump is the president,

00:20:18.814 --> 00:20:21.084
the power he holds over Republicans

00:20:21.084 --> 00:20:23.767
in his party is tremendous.

00:20:23.767 --> 00:20:27.841
And you know as Jared Kushner says,

00:20:27.841 --> 00:20:30.204
"this is Donald Trump's Republican party."

00:20:30.204 --> 00:20:33.124
And so if you're a Republican
member of the House or Senate,

00:20:33.124 --> 00:20:37.344
you are judged by your
president and also the partisans

00:20:37.344 --> 00:20:42.344
in your party by whether or
not you support Donald Trump.

00:20:43.234 --> 00:20:46.174
Well, that's not really the way(laughs)

00:20:46.174 --> 00:20:48.944
the founders intended to operate.

00:20:48.944 --> 00:20:52.074
They wanted a system in which
lawmakers use their reason

00:20:52.074 --> 00:20:54.187
to evaluate the questions of the day

00:20:54.187 --> 00:20:55.910
and political affiliation

00:20:55.910 --> 00:21:00.061
and maintaining political
power was what they feared.

00:21:00.061 --> 00:21:02.554
But the system is where it is.

00:21:02.554 --> 00:21:06.754
So what you need is something
to happen that breaks the link

00:21:06.754 --> 00:21:08.884
between individual members of a party

00:21:08.884 --> 00:21:11.294
and the group interest of that party.

00:21:11.294 --> 00:21:16.294
That basically says to people,
"geez just supporting my team

00:21:16.324 --> 00:21:19.234
has caused this enormous electoral loss.

00:21:19.234 --> 00:21:21.804
I should probably go back to what

00:21:21.804 --> 00:21:23.544
the people want me to care about,"

00:21:23.544 --> 00:21:26.092
which is whatever it happens to be.

00:21:26.092 --> 00:21:29.954
And so you sever the extreme adhesion

00:21:29.954 --> 00:21:32.674
between individual
candidates and their parties.

00:21:32.674 --> 00:21:35.374
One of the ways you do that is,

00:21:35.374 --> 00:21:38.104
and that has to probably
happen through an event.

00:21:38.104 --> 00:21:40.734
In other words a massive electoral loss.

00:21:40.734 --> 00:21:44.014
It would then potentially
open the door to some reforms.

00:21:44.014 --> 00:21:46.384
And what the reforms would
do is they would break down

00:21:46.384 --> 00:21:48.676
the structural impediments
to bipartisanship,

00:21:48.676 --> 00:21:50.984
which I won't go into,

00:21:50.984 --> 00:21:53.084
but which I go to into in
some length in my book.

00:21:53.084 --> 00:21:55.574
But which the death of
split ticket voting,

00:21:55.574 --> 00:21:57.484
the fact that we all live near each other

00:21:57.484 --> 00:21:59.474
and therefore, if you're a Republican,

00:21:59.474 --> 00:22:01.874
you tend to live on a block
full of Republicans now

00:22:01.874 --> 00:22:04.884
in a way that you didn't in the seventies.

00:22:04.884 --> 00:22:09.004
Gerrymandering all kinds
of other structural parts

00:22:09.004 --> 00:22:12.449
of the system that
accentuate partisanship,

00:22:12.449 --> 00:22:15.564
you would need something to
basically have some reforms

00:22:15.564 --> 00:22:18.796
that would break apart those
structural sorting techniques

00:22:18.796 --> 00:22:21.954
that have created, or I
shouldn't say they've,

00:22:21.954 --> 00:22:24.454
they both created and
locked in the partisanship

00:22:24.454 --> 00:22:26.022
we have now.

00:22:26.022 --> 00:22:28.920
- Yeah, no I mean, it's
a fascinating thing

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:30.234
of how it could evolve,

00:22:30.234 --> 00:22:31.404
but that makes a ton of sense.

00:22:31.404 --> 00:22:34.424
Going to your book, "The
Hardest Job In The World"

00:22:34.424 --> 00:22:36.074
were you're referring to the presidency,

00:22:36.074 --> 00:22:37.264
I can think of other hard jobs too,

00:22:37.264 --> 00:22:39.254
but that one does seem hard.

00:22:39.254 --> 00:22:40.921
From YouTube niche786 says,

00:22:40.921 --> 00:22:43.594
"you make the case in "The
Hardest Job In The World"

00:22:43.594 --> 00:22:45.484
that the demands of the
American presidency have grown

00:22:45.484 --> 00:22:47.816
impossible for any one person to fulfill.

00:22:47.816 --> 00:22:49.864
What problems does that pose?"

00:22:49.864 --> 00:22:51.778
And I'll add to niches's question,

00:22:51.778 --> 00:22:53.374
how do you fix that?

00:22:53.374 --> 00:22:56.794
I mean, it sounds like a
pretty hard thing to fix.

00:22:56.794 --> 00:22:58.615
- Well thank you for the question

00:22:58.615 --> 00:23:00.045
and thanks for reading the book.

00:23:00.045 --> 00:23:02.854
So we were talking about
just some of the problems

00:23:02.854 --> 00:23:05.575
with the modern presidency,
which is partisanship,

00:23:05.575 --> 00:23:07.594
which has locked up Congress,

00:23:07.594 --> 00:23:09.144
which means that the president no longer

00:23:09.144 --> 00:23:10.734
has a willing partner

00:23:10.734 --> 00:23:13.054
or no longer has the
willing partner of the kind

00:23:13.054 --> 00:23:15.536
that the founders wanted in Congress.

00:23:15.536 --> 00:23:19.420
Something that operates
independently to address

00:23:19.420 --> 00:23:22.498
the big challenges of the day
in a way that accommodates

00:23:22.498 --> 00:23:24.462
the diversity of the country.

00:23:24.462 --> 00:23:26.947
What we have now is as Congress

00:23:26.947 --> 00:23:30.974
is less able to do its job effectively,

00:23:30.974 --> 00:23:33.024
a lot of the decision-making
gets thrown over

00:23:33.024 --> 00:23:34.454
onto the president's plate.

00:23:34.454 --> 00:23:35.934
And then the president has to use

00:23:35.934 --> 00:23:37.334
the weak tools of the office,

00:23:37.334 --> 00:23:39.894
which are not universally
applicable to all the problems of

00:23:39.894 --> 00:23:43.104
the day to through executive
orders and other ways to try

00:23:43.104 --> 00:23:45.884
to address some of the issues
that Americans care about.

00:23:45.884 --> 00:23:48.004
Well, executive orders
and executive action

00:23:48.004 --> 00:23:50.052
and work through the
administrative agencies

00:23:50.052 --> 00:23:51.844
is a weak way to do it,

00:23:51.844 --> 00:23:54.964
basically it doesn't get the job done.

00:23:54.964 --> 00:23:59.964
And when presidents act in ways
that are seen as unilateral

00:24:00.334 --> 00:24:02.404
by the opposite political party,

00:24:02.404 --> 00:24:06.224
it encourages the opposite
political party to be very angry.

00:24:06.224 --> 00:24:08.084
If you can work things through Congress,

00:24:08.084 --> 00:24:09.934
the opposite political
party at least has a voice

00:24:09.934 --> 00:24:10.954
and a structure.

00:24:10.954 --> 00:24:13.254
Now some people would argue
about that with respect

00:24:13.254 --> 00:24:16.264
to the recent Supreme court
fights that have gone on.

00:24:16.264 --> 00:24:19.209
But nevertheless, when Congress
is working as it's intended,

00:24:19.209 --> 00:24:22.454
it's basically a structure
for having arguments

00:24:22.454 --> 00:24:24.634
and for seeing who wins that argument,

00:24:24.634 --> 00:24:27.834
and then at least making
the losers feel like

00:24:27.834 --> 00:24:29.894
the system was fair
enough so that they'll,

00:24:29.894 --> 00:24:31.664
they can live to fight another day.

00:24:31.664 --> 00:24:34.234
What you don't want in any
system is where people feel like

00:24:34.234 --> 00:24:37.834
they were basically
maneuvered by the structure

00:24:37.834 --> 00:24:39.834
of the system to a bad result,

00:24:39.834 --> 00:24:42.034
and they really couldn't change anything.

00:24:42.034 --> 00:24:44.284
So some of the structural
changes in Congress

00:24:44.284 --> 00:24:46.104
we've been talking
about, we need to change.

00:24:46.104 --> 00:24:50.085
The president, has accumulated
power ever since basically

00:24:50.085 --> 00:24:53.204
you can put finger down
anywhere in the timeline,

00:24:53.204 --> 00:24:55.174
but let's say basically the new deal

00:24:55.174 --> 00:24:56.734
and the second world war.

00:24:56.734 --> 00:24:59.184
So that's hard power to give back

00:24:59.184 --> 00:25:00.284
and they don't want to do that.

00:25:00.284 --> 00:25:02.764
But as Americans, we can
stop looking to the president

00:25:02.764 --> 00:25:05.244
every time we want a political solution.

00:25:05.244 --> 00:25:08.554
And we can stop rewarding
presidents who promised

00:25:08.554 --> 00:25:11.233
that they alone can do it
as Donald Trump promised.

00:25:11.233 --> 00:25:14.958
You need to elevate presidents who operate

00:25:14.958 --> 00:25:17.834
in the American system as it should work,

00:25:17.834 --> 00:25:19.684
not as an American system

00:25:19.684 --> 00:25:21.924
in which they are the
lone superpower, sorry,

00:25:21.924 --> 00:25:25.534
the lone superhero who can
through force of their will

00:25:25.534 --> 00:25:27.874
and skill solve all of America's problems.

00:25:27.874 --> 00:25:29.494
It's not the way the
government is supposed to work

00:25:29.494 --> 00:25:30.484
and we should stop thinking

00:25:30.484 --> 00:25:32.104
that's the way it's supposed to work,

00:25:32.104 --> 00:25:33.904
'cause it just leads to frustration.

00:25:34.844 --> 00:25:36.294
- Yeah although I wonder about that.

00:25:36.294 --> 00:25:38.754
I mean, even in corporate
America we tend to have,

00:25:38.754 --> 00:25:40.644
you know the CEO, we,

00:25:40.644 --> 00:25:43.304
Elon Musk is sending rockets to space.

00:25:43.304 --> 00:25:45.214
Well no it's Elon Musk
plus a lot of other people

00:25:45.214 --> 00:25:46.949
are doing it.(laughs)

00:25:46.949 --> 00:25:50.404
Steve jobs, and a lot of other people.

00:25:50.404 --> 00:25:52.954
- Well but in the corporate
world at least that's,

00:25:52.954 --> 00:25:55.104
there's the chance that
that's more likely to happen

00:25:55.104 --> 00:25:58.114
because the CEO can have absolute power

00:25:58.114 --> 00:26:00.204
if they've designed the
organization that way.

00:26:00.204 --> 00:26:01.974
Even if a president wanted absolute power,

00:26:01.974 --> 00:26:03.266
they don't have it.

00:26:03.266 --> 00:26:07.544
And they, it's sort of
the worst of both worlds,

00:26:07.544 --> 00:26:10.244
because if we think of them
as an all-powerful CEO,

00:26:10.244 --> 00:26:12.994
but then don't give them
the tools to do the job,

00:26:12.994 --> 00:26:15.314
then they both make claims

00:26:15.314 --> 00:26:17.284
like they're gonna be the
Elon Musk of the government,

00:26:17.284 --> 00:26:19.941
but then don't have the ability to do it.

00:26:19.941 --> 00:26:23.058
And, there's not really
a feedback mechanism

00:26:23.058 --> 00:26:25.505
that there is in the corporate world,

00:26:25.505 --> 00:26:27.044
basically with the presidency,

00:26:27.044 --> 00:26:29.440
there's one feedback
mechanism, reelection.

00:26:29.440 --> 00:26:34.114
And most of them take
intermediate feedback mechanisms

00:26:34.114 --> 00:26:36.877
if they lose a midterm or
if their polls go down.

00:26:36.877 --> 00:26:39.630
But if a president has a
strong enough pain threshold

00:26:39.630 --> 00:26:41.789
or a high enough pain
threshold, I should say,

00:26:41.789 --> 00:26:46.789
they can power through
and face no real feedback

00:26:47.471 --> 00:26:51.224
other than other than the
election after four years,

00:26:51.224 --> 00:26:54.684
because Congress is no
longer a feedback mechanism.

00:26:54.684 --> 00:26:56.684
Because everything is so connected now,

00:26:56.684 --> 00:27:00.494
if you are a Senator, basically
as a Republican Senator,

00:27:00.494 --> 00:27:02.274
if you're running your voters wanna know

00:27:02.274 --> 00:27:04.614
are you for the president or against him.

00:27:04.614 --> 00:27:05.944
The rest is secondary.

00:27:05.944 --> 00:27:07.894
And that's I say that
for having interviewed

00:27:07.894 --> 00:27:09.744
a lot of Republican senators running.

00:27:11.894 --> 00:27:13.074
- I could talk to you for hours,

00:27:13.074 --> 00:27:15.764
especially given the world
that we're in right now,

00:27:15.764 --> 00:27:17.166
but just in the last question,

00:27:17.166 --> 00:27:21.410
as a member of the media
and the type of journalism

00:27:21.410 --> 00:27:24.194
I've followed you many years,
even before we first met.

00:27:24.194 --> 00:27:26.094
And we've had many
conversations like this,

00:27:26.094 --> 00:27:28.254
even when we were non
cameras, I have deep respect.

00:27:28.254 --> 00:27:29.454
You know you're kind
of like the journalist

00:27:29.454 --> 00:27:31.084
that I always imagined, like the people

00:27:31.084 --> 00:27:33.404
there trying to be the truth
seekers and they're reading

00:27:33.404 --> 00:27:35.714
the history books and they're
writing the history books.

00:27:35.714 --> 00:27:39.014
What do you, what advice would
you have for kids out there

00:27:39.014 --> 00:27:41.101
who might be watching or
people of any age whose like

00:27:41.101 --> 00:27:42.884
"well, you know we're not,

00:27:42.884 --> 00:27:44.834
we're past Walter Cronkite somehow

00:27:44.834 --> 00:27:47.416
and the fourth estate of the
journalism and social media

00:27:47.416 --> 00:27:48.714
has somehow ruined everything.

00:27:48.714 --> 00:27:52.174
And, there isn't, there's
everyone's version

00:27:52.174 --> 00:27:53.024
of the truth now."

00:27:53.024 --> 00:27:54.524
So what advice would you have to someone

00:27:54.524 --> 00:27:55.754
who wants to become a journalist

00:27:55.754 --> 00:27:57.854
and why should they be excited about that?

00:27:58.864 --> 00:28:01.421
- Well, I think that my advice would be,

00:28:01.421 --> 00:28:03.273
and it goes back to Khan Academy.

00:28:03.273 --> 00:28:06.934
What is one of the great things
about what you've created?

00:28:06.934 --> 00:28:09.981
It is to allow people
who are curious to learn

00:28:09.981 --> 00:28:13.604
and to follow that curiosity.

00:28:13.604 --> 00:28:16.094
And in the process of
following that curiosity,

00:28:16.094 --> 00:28:17.658
develop the school, the skills

00:28:17.658 --> 00:28:19.384
for understanding their world.

00:28:19.384 --> 00:28:20.707
So critical thinking,

00:28:21.710 --> 00:28:26.114
and curiosity and critical
thinking are necessary

00:28:26.114 --> 00:28:29.344
to live a meaningful life, I think.

00:28:29.344 --> 00:28:30.344
It turns out also

00:28:30.344 --> 00:28:33.964
that they are what should
fuel you as a journalist.

00:28:33.964 --> 00:28:36.314
And so one of the things I think,

00:28:36.314 --> 00:28:38.944
whether you're interested
in journalism or not,

00:28:38.944 --> 00:28:40.714
seeking out your own answers,

00:28:40.714 --> 00:28:42.824
following your own curiosity,

00:28:42.824 --> 00:28:47.446
not just what happened, but
why it happened for yourself,

00:28:47.446 --> 00:28:49.824
not because you're skeptical
and you think everybody's

00:28:49.824 --> 00:28:51.004
trying to lie to you,

00:28:51.004 --> 00:28:53.054
but because you know
that when you investigate

00:28:53.054 --> 00:28:56.364
the why of things, when you
go look it up on your own,

00:28:56.364 --> 00:28:59.754
when you follow your own
path of your curiosity,

00:28:59.754 --> 00:29:00.764
it's rewarding.

00:29:00.764 --> 00:29:03.164
And you gain, in a democratic system,

00:29:03.164 --> 00:29:05.334
you gain control over
the world around you,

00:29:05.334 --> 00:29:07.104
by understanding what's happening.

00:29:07.104 --> 00:29:09.284
Events don't come plopping on you,

00:29:09.284 --> 00:29:11.504
you understand what's happening

00:29:11.504 --> 00:29:14.594
and so you feel some
control in a frantic world

00:29:14.594 --> 00:29:17.748
where things can seem out of control.

00:29:17.748 --> 00:29:21.654
And so what I would say
is anybody who is curious

00:29:21.654 --> 00:29:24.196
and a critical thinker
can then go get a job

00:29:24.196 --> 00:29:27.139
where you have a license to be curious,

00:29:27.139 --> 00:29:30.069
and hopefully use those
critical thinking skills

00:29:30.069 --> 00:29:33.794
to follow your curiosity.

00:29:33.794 --> 00:29:35.454
The final thing I would say is for anybody

00:29:35.454 --> 00:29:37.258
who wants to become a journalist

00:29:37.258 --> 00:29:38.704
or anyone who wants to
engage in journalism,

00:29:38.704 --> 00:29:42.454
just like if they're
learning through Khan Academy

00:29:42.454 --> 00:29:44.964
or any other learning process,

00:29:44.964 --> 00:29:49.074
experimentation and understanding
is not a straight line.

00:29:49.074 --> 00:29:51.574
There are things that we once
believed about the movement of

00:29:51.574 --> 00:29:52.774
the planets and the heavens

00:29:52.774 --> 00:29:55.304
that we've now changed our thinking about.

00:29:55.304 --> 00:29:58.264
Einstein was a genius who saw ahead of

00:29:58.264 --> 00:30:02.371
entire generations of thinkers,
but he also got a lot wrong.

00:30:02.371 --> 00:30:05.694
And he, when he found out he
had gotten something wrong,

00:30:05.694 --> 00:30:07.724
he didn't feel glum about it,

00:30:07.724 --> 00:30:09.074
he was delighted because it meant

00:30:09.074 --> 00:30:10.914
that he now knew the right answer.

00:30:10.914 --> 00:30:14.144
He now could pursue a whole
new set of curiosities.

00:30:14.144 --> 00:30:15.618
And I think as consumers of journalism

00:30:15.618 --> 00:30:19.244
and of any information is to
understand that being wrong

00:30:19.244 --> 00:30:23.674
is not always the product of
somebody who has ill intent

00:30:23.674 --> 00:30:26.204
or somebody who is acting in bad faith.

00:30:26.204 --> 00:30:28.724
There are plenty of
those people to be sure,

00:30:28.724 --> 00:30:30.864
but if we understand that
the pursuit of knowledge

00:30:30.864 --> 00:30:34.864
is sometimes a crooked path,
then we won't get all undone

00:30:34.864 --> 00:30:36.424
every time we learn something

00:30:36.424 --> 00:30:39.074
that we thought might not
be true the day before.

00:30:39.074 --> 00:30:40.944
When we, accumulation of knowledge

00:30:40.944 --> 00:30:42.254
is a kind of crooked path.

00:30:42.254 --> 00:30:43.224
And we need to know that,

00:30:43.224 --> 00:30:45.254
and we need to know that
about our journalism.

00:30:45.254 --> 00:30:47.804
In its best form it's self-correcting,

00:30:47.804 --> 00:30:49.634
the more you learn, the more you learn

00:30:49.634 --> 00:30:51.784
and the better informed you are.

00:30:51.784 --> 00:30:54.954
But we should make sure
we think of it in that way

00:30:54.954 --> 00:30:55.921
and don't always think,

00:30:55.921 --> 00:30:58.874
"Oh well, they said it
was gonna be this way

00:30:58.874 --> 00:31:00.384
and it turned out to be that way.

00:31:00.384 --> 00:31:03.504
And therefore, everything they
always say is without merit."

00:31:03.504 --> 00:31:06.494
That's not true in life and
we shouldn't think about it

00:31:06.494 --> 00:31:09.934
when it's not true in the way
we receive our information.

00:31:09.934 --> 00:31:12.874
A little more understanding
of the way we receive our

00:31:12.874 --> 00:31:15.607
information will help us
to be better informed.

00:31:16.974 --> 00:31:17.807
- No, I love that.

00:31:17.807 --> 00:31:20.964
And that seems applicable to
journalism, the media and life.

00:31:20.964 --> 00:31:22.984
To just be constantly truth seeking

00:31:22.984 --> 00:31:25.134
and not tying your identity, your ego

00:31:25.134 --> 00:31:27.414
to a certain set current fact base,

00:31:27.414 --> 00:31:29.534
and always be open to new ideas.

00:31:29.534 --> 00:31:32.034
John thank you so much,
this was a real treat

00:31:32.034 --> 00:31:33.314
especially given the timing.

00:31:33.314 --> 00:31:34.644
Hopefully we can debrief

00:31:34.644 --> 00:31:37.394
and see how correct your prognostications

00:31:37.394 --> 00:31:40.154
were in a couple weeks or
months.(Sal laughs)(John laughs)

00:31:40.154 --> 00:31:41.684
- Well, I would love to do it,

00:31:41.684 --> 00:31:43.464
and it's always a pleasure to be with you.

00:31:43.464 --> 00:31:45.464
So thanks for giving me the opportunity.

00:31:47.034 --> 00:31:48.517
- Thank you John.

00:31:48.517 --> 00:31:49.984
- See you Sal.

00:31:49.984 --> 00:31:53.434
- So thanks everyone for joining,

00:31:53.434 --> 00:31:55.184
always a fun conversation.

00:31:55.184 --> 00:31:57.141
You know John really is
one of those journalists,

00:31:57.141 --> 00:31:59.084
I've had the pleasure of getting

00:31:59.084 --> 00:32:01.084
to have kind of just random
conversations with them

00:32:01.084 --> 00:32:02.944
that go a lot like the
conversation we just had,

00:32:02.944 --> 00:32:06.344
but he definitely gives you
a spark of curiosity around,

00:32:06.344 --> 00:32:10.884
the messiness but also the beauty

00:32:10.884 --> 00:32:12.501
of what makes a society a society,

00:32:12.501 --> 00:32:14.844
what makes a government a government.

00:32:14.844 --> 00:32:16.844
And I really do think he's,

00:32:16.844 --> 00:32:21.844
that form of journalism
that we need a lot more of,

00:32:22.132 --> 00:32:23.894
more than ever.

00:32:23.894 --> 00:32:26.784
But just as a reminder for tomorrow,

00:32:26.784 --> 00:32:28.234
another really great guest,

00:32:28.234 --> 00:32:31.684
we're gonna have the Vivek
Murthy, former surgeon general

00:32:31.684 --> 00:32:34.424
and kind of talk about, as you imagine

00:32:34.424 --> 00:32:36.394
a lot of things healthcare,

00:32:36.394 --> 00:32:38.083
what's going on with COVID

00:32:38.083 --> 00:32:41.297
and probably a little bit of,

00:32:41.297 --> 00:32:43.734
what the surgeon general does
in the role of government

00:32:43.734 --> 00:32:44.634
and all of this as well.

00:32:44.634 --> 00:32:47.374
So I'll look forward to
having that conversation.

00:32:47.374 --> 00:32:51.074
So please join us tomorrow
with, on the Homeroom same time.

00:32:51.074 --> 00:32:52.174
Talk to you all later.

